Description: Builds multimodal transport networks from 'OpenStreetMap'
    extracts, 'GTFS' feeds and, optionally, an elevation raster, and routes
    on them by walking, cycling, driving and public transport. The routing
    engine is a 'Rust' port of Conveyal's 'R5' routing engine, Conway, Byrd
    and van der Linden (2017) <doi:10.3141/2653-06>, and the functions are
    modelled on those of the 'r5r' package, Pereira, Saraiva, Herszenhut,
    Braga and Conway (2021) <doi:10.32866/001c.21262>, from whose 'Java'
    code the fare calculator and parts of the trip planner are translated.
    Computes travel-time matrices, accessibility to opportunities,
    isochrones, detailed itineraries and trip options that trade off travel
    time, fare, transfers and walking. Supports fare structures, slope-aware
    walking and cycling, and scenarios of car speeds and cycling stress
    applied per call without rebuilding the network. The engine runs inside
    the R session with no 'Java' runtime, keeps the network in memory, uses
    all cores, and is shared with the 'Python' and 'JavaScript' packages of
    the same name. The methods include the round-based public transport
    routing of Delling, Pajor and Werneck (2015)
    <doi:10.1287/trsc.2014.0534>, the travel-time percentiles of Conway,
    Byrd and van Eggermond (2018)
    <doi:10.5198/jtlu.2018.1074>, the fare-constrained routing of Conway
    and Stewart (2019) <doi:10.1080/13658816.2019.1605075> and the
    contraction hierarchies of Geisberger, Sanders, Schultes and Vetter
    (2012) <doi:10.1287/trsc.1110.0401>.
